## Running the Definition file
The `octo.yaml` is your Octo definition file.
It contains all the modules you want to run,
which will create your infrastructure resources as per the module's instructions.

Octo generates Terragrunt module folders from your definition; Terraform/Tofu (via Terragrunt) is the
executor. The flow has three steps — generate, apply, then commit the results back into Octo. An
optional validate step compares the Terraform plan against Octo's own diff.

Each step is a script, so there is nothing to set up first:

```bash
# 1. Generate the Terragrunt module folders into ./.octo-tf (folders are overwritten in place; state and unrelated folders survive).
npm run octo:generate

# 2. (optional) Plan every module folder and validate the plans against Octo's resource diff.
#    `validate` runs the plan itself; there is nothing to run beforehand.
npm run octo:validate

# 3. Take the app lock. Apply and commit both require it, so that only one run writes state at a time.
LOCK_ID=$(npm run --silent octo:lock)

# 4. Apply, then record the applied outputs into Octo. The commit releases the lock.
npm run octo:apply -- --lockId $LOCK_ID
npm run octo:commit -- --lockId $LOCK_ID
```

Generate and validate need no lock — neither writes shared state. Apply and commit do, and refuse to
run without one. The commit releases it however it ends, so a normal run leaves nothing behind. If a
run dies between the lock and the commit, `npm run octo:unlock -- --force` releases it.

If the apply fails partway, `npm run octo:commit -- --lockId $LOCK_ID --partial` records what did
apply instead of rejecting the whole commit, leaving the resources it could not confirm out of Octo's
state so the next run creates them again.

`octo apply` plans first and refuses to proceed if the apply would destroy anything, printing what
would be destroyed. Run `npm run octo:validate` to see why each one is being destroyed, then re-run
the apply with `--auto-approve` to go ahead — `npm run octo:apply -- --auto-approve`. That flag is
required for every destroy, including the teardown flow below.

Each script sets `OCTO_DEFINITION_FILE_PATH=octo.yaml` and points at `.octo-tf` for you. Edit the
scripts in `package.json` if your definition file or output directory is named differently. Running
the commands by hand works too — `node_modules/.bin/octo generate -o .octo-tf` with that variable
set — but the scripts are what keep the steps agreeing with each other.

> `octo run-action` is invoked automatically by Terraform mid-apply for resources whose lifecycle runs
> outside Terraform; you do not call it directly. `octo apply` hands it the definition file and the
> `octo` binary it needs, so it works the same whether you use the scripts or not.

## Development
The rest of the setup is a TypeScript project for you to get started with developing new modules in Octo quickly.
You can follow the official documentation to learn more.
Once you have your custom constructs and modules defined, you can use below commands to build those modules.
Don't forget to add your new modules to your Octo definition file.

```bash
npm run build
```

## Destroy
Once you have your infrastructure created, and you wish all of them destroyed,
its as simple as commenting out the necessary modules and re-running the generate → apply → commit flow.
Octo rewrites a removed module's folder as an **empty** module — no resources, but the Terraform
configuration and providers intact — so Terragrunt still discovers it and destroys its resources on
the next apply. (The folder is never deleted: an emptied folder is what makes the destroy happen.)
After the destroy is committed, the leftover empty folder is benign — Octo warns about it and you can
delete it by hand.

### Step 1
In `octo.yaml`, comment out all modules except the `app` and `account` modules.
The app and account modules load AWS credentials to call AWS APIs, hence they remain uncommented.

By commenting the `s3-website-service-module` module below,
you are safely deleting the s3-website resources created by this module.

```yaml
modules:
  - moduleId: app-module
    moduleClass: SimpleAppModule
    moduleInputs:
      name: aws-s3-website

  - moduleId: account-module
    moduleClass: AwsIniAccountModule
    moduleInputs:
      accountId: "${env.AWS_ACCOUNT_ID}"
      app: "${{app-module.model.app}}"

  #- moduleId: s3-website-service-module
  #  moduleClass: AwsS3StaticWebsiteServiceModule
  #  moduleInputs:
  #    account: "${{account-module.model.account}}"
  #    awsRegionId: us-east-1
  #    bucketName: "${env.AWS_S3_WEBSITE_BUCKET_NAME}"
  #    directoryPath: website
```

### Step 2
Then, re-build (if necessary) and re-run the generate → apply → commit flow from "Running the Definition file",
passing `--auto-approve` to the apply step - a teardown destroys resources, and `octo apply` refuses to
destroy anything without it.

```bash
npm run octo:generate
LOCK_ID=$(npm run --silent octo:lock)
npm run octo:apply -- --lockId $LOCK_ID --auto-approve
npm run octo:commit -- --lockId $LOCK_ID
```

The commit is not optional here. A resource whose lifecycle runs outside Terraform is deleted by its
own delete action, and `octo commit` is what runs it — Terraform cannot, because the resource's
configuration is gone by the time it would.
